rts. Board has zero hearts. So you have two hearts, need three more. Flop has no hearts → so only two hearts so far. So flush draw is *backdoor* — needs turn and river both hearts. Very low probability (~4.5% to make flush by river). Not a strong draw.\n\nAlso, straight draws: you have 9,2. Board: 6,5,K. To make a straight, you need 7,8 or 3,4 etc. With 9,2, you can only make 2-3-4-5-6 (wheel) if 3,4,7 appear? No — you need five consecutive. Best hope: 3,4,7,8 — too many missing. Actually, with 9 and 2, you have no straight draw. 2 and 9 are too far apart.\n\nSo your hand has **no pair, no draw** — just two undercards, one of which is a 9, the other a 2. The board has K, 6, 5. You have no overcards except 9 < K, so not even overcard.\n\nYou have **essentially nothing**.\n\nOpponent raised on the flop — strong indication of strength: likely Kx, 6x, 5x, or a draw like 7-8, or a set.\n\nYour hand is very weak.\n\nHowever — you are only being asked to call 104 into a pot. Your stack is 19,900, so 104 is ~0.5% of your stack.\n\nBut implied odds? Even if you hit a 9 (one pair), it will likely not be good. Two pair? Impossible with current board. Three of a kind? Only if a 9 hits — but 9 is low, and board has K, so dominated.\n\nFlush is extremely unlikely (needs two hearts).\n\nThus, **calling is speculative but very low EV**.\n\nHowever, the cost is tiny relative to stack and pot.\n\nBut let’s consider: what was the big blind? From pre-flop both checked, then opponent raises 104 on flop — likely big blind is around 100 or so. Assume BB = 100.\n\nThen 104 is a small raise. You’re getting good pot odds to call with any draw — but you have almost no draw.\n\nStill, with such a small price and deep stacks, some players call with backdoor draws in position.\n\nBut you have no position info — assume you act after.\n\nBut key point: you are in the lead (19,900 vs likely opponent similar), and folding here preserves chips.\n\nBut losing 104 on a bad hand occasionally is acceptab