t by 228. But phrasing is “raised bet by 228”.\n\nGiven the valid actions include 0, 456, etc., and 456 is *twice* 228, this suggests that **to raise**, you must bet at least twice the previous raise (2×228 = 456), per standard poker rules.\n\nTherefore:\n- **0** = Call the 228 bet\n- **456+** = Raise (minimum raise is 456 total additional bet)\n- **-1** = Fold\n- **-2** = All-in\n\nSo you must either:\n- Fold (-1)\n- Call 228 (action 0)\n- Raise to at least 456\n- All-in\n\nYour hand is **K-5 offsuit**, board is **A-10-8**, coordinated for straights (J-9 possible). You have no pair, no strong draw. King is dominated by any Ace. Even K-10 is better than you.\n\nCalling 228 chips to see the turn with a weak high card and no real draw is risky. Opponent likely has a strong hand (A-x, T-x, or overpairs). Raising would represent strength you don’t have — a bluff, but against an aggressive player who raised first, it\'s dangerous.\n\nBest move: **Fold or Call?**\n\nYou’re in chip lead (19,900 left), so survival matters. But folding too easily lets opponent push you around.\n\nHowever, **this hand has very low equity**. No pair, weak kicker, no draw. Calling gives you ~12 outs *only if* you hit a King (3 outs) — but even then, A-K beats you if opponent has A-x. And you’ll be out of position.\n\nEstimated equity: <20% against likely range (A-x, 10-x, A-A, T-T, etc.)\n\nCalling 228 to potentially win maybe 500–600 total pot is not worth the risk with such weak hand.\n\nRaising (bluff) is possible but unlikely to succeed — opponent already showed strength. Minimum raise is 456, which is large relative to pot.\n\nAll-in is overkill — you’d be committing more than half your stack bluffing.\n\nBest decision: **Fold**\n\nBut wait — did you already put money in? Pre-flop both checked. So pot is small. Opponent raised 228 on flop. To call: 228. Pot odds? Unknown pot size, but likely small.\n\nEven with 3:1 odds, you need ~25% equity to call — you don’t have it.\n\nReason:  \nMy hand is weak (King-high), the board is coordinated, and my opponent has shown strength by raising on the flop. I have no pair, no strong draw, and little chance to improve. Continuing in this hand risks more chips with poor equity. Given my chip lead, preserving chips is more important than hero calls.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
