eak pair or high card. The only possible strong hands here would involve the paired 2s on the board. Your opponent has gone all-in (-2) on the turn, which signals extreme strength or a bluff with a polarized range. However, given the lack of draws (no flush or straight draw completing yet), and the fact that you only have 9-high with no pair, your hand is almost certainly behind.\n\nPossible hands your opponent could have:\n- Two Pair (J-J + 2-2 or J-x with a second J)\n- A set (2-2-x or J-J-x)\n- Overpairs (A-A, K-K, Q-Q, J-J)\n- Bluff with missed draw (less likely on this board)\n\nBut critically, you have **no pair**, not even a jack. Your best possible high card is 9, which cannot win against any reasonable calling range. Even if the river were to pair your 9, it would still be unlikely to beat two pair or better.\n\nYour valid actions are -2 (call all-in) or -1 (fold).\n\nCalling all-in means committing your remaining 19,900 chips with essentially no hand. Given the opponent’s all-in action on the turn — a strong and committed move — and your extremely weak holdings, this is almost certainly a losing call.\n\nReason:  \nFolding is the only rational option. You have no pair, no draw, and no showdown value. Calling an all-in from an opponent who likely has at least two pair or a set would be throwing away chips unnecessarily. Since you're already in the lead overall and have a healthy stack, preserving chips is crucial — especially with only 3 hands left in the game.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
