ing (-1)**: Too strong a hand to fold. Two pair with a redrawn board is usually good enough to call a small river bet.\n\n- **Raising (774+)**: Would represent extreme strength — like a full house or better. But you don’t have that. Bluff-raising here is risky, especially since opponent initiated action. They likely have a strong hand or a bluff. But your hand doesn’t block much.\n\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill. You’re risking ~20k to win a tiny pot. Not justifiable.\n\nThe key insight: **You have a medium-strength hand on the river. Best play is to call and showdown.**\n\nOpponent may have:\n- Flush (any ♦ with J, 10, 9, K, A)\n- Full house (if they had 2-8, 2-Q, 8-8, etc.)\n- Two pair (Q-8, Q-2, etc.)\n\nBut also possible they’re bluffing with air or missed draw.\n\nSince the bet is small (only 387), and you have 19,900 left, **the price is right to call**.\n\nRaising only loses to better hands and wins nothing from worse hands — no worse hand will call, and better hands will continue.\n\nTherefore, **calling is optimal**.\n\nReason:  \nThe pot odds and hand strength justify a call. Folding loses equity unnecessarily. Raising or going all-in is overly aggressive with a medium-strength hand. Calling realizes equity against bluffs or weaker made hands.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
